.Dd September 28, 2026
.Dt MKDIR 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mkdir ,
.Nm mkdirat ,
.Nm rmdir
.Nd create or remove a directory
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/stat.h
.Ft int
.Fn mkdir "const char *path" "mode_t mode"
.Ft int
.Fn mkdirat "int dirfd" "const char *path" "mode_t mode"
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn rmdir "const char *path"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn mkdir
creates the directory
.Fa path .
.Fn mkdirat
does the same with a relative
.Fa path
resolved from the directory
.Fa dirfd
is open on, or from the current working directory for
.Dv AT_FDCWD ;
an absolute path ignores
.Fa dirfd .
.Xr unlinkat 2
with
.Dv AT_REMOVEDIR
is the matching form of
.Fn rmdir .
Its parent must exist and the final name must not already exist.
Creating the root directory or a mount point returns
.Er EEXIST .
A slash after the final name is allowed.
A last component of
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&..
returns
.Er EEXIST
once the directory before it is walked, which returns
.Er ENOENT
when it is missing, as on Linux; nothing is made.
.Pp
On KRFFS and ext2, the new directory receives the caller's effective
user and group IDs and the permission bits
.Li "mode & 07777 & ~umask" .
FAT32 and exFAT ignore the requested mode and use the mount's user ID,
group ID, and mask.
Their reported permissions are 0777 with the mount mask removed;
the read-only attribute removes write permission.
.Pp
A KRFFS directory's identifier, by which its children name it, is its
parent's mixed with the 64-bit hash of its name, and two names can have
one hash:
.Pa bC
and
.Pa cb
do.
The second of two such names in one directory is refused with
.Er EINVAL ,
the answer for a name the file system cannot hold, since the two
directories would share their children.
Linux has no such refusal.
.Pp
.Fn rmdir
removes an empty directory without following a symbolic link in the
final component.
It cannot remove a mount point, the root directory, or a directory that
is any process's root directory, which
.Xr chroot 2
set.
Linux refuses the first two only, the root by the path that names it:
it removes a directory some process has as its root, the caller's own
among them when the caller names it from outside, since
.Xr chroot 2
leaves the working directory where it was.
A nonempty directory returns
.Er ENOTEMPTY .
The root returns
.Er EBUSY ,
a path whose last component is
.Pa \&.
returns
.Er EINVAL ,
and one whose last component is
.Pa \&..
returns
.Er ENOTEMPTY ,
once the directories before it are walked and whatever the directory
holds, as on Linux.
A slash after the final name is allowed, and names the directory; a
symbolic link so named is not followed and is refused as a
non-directory.
On a mounted KRFFS volume, a missing final entry or a final entry that
is not a directory also returns
.Er ENOTEMPTY .
.Pp
A directory in use is not removed:
.Fn rmdir
returns
.Er EBUSY .
On KRFFS, the boot root and a mounted volume alike, a directory is in
use while a descriptor refers to it, an
.Dv O_PATH
one or one in flight on a socket included, or while it is a process's
working directory, and the refusal comes before the check for
emptiness: KRFFS keeps no node for such a holder, and a freed directory's
place would go to the next node made while the holder still refers to it.
ext2, FAT32 and exFAT refuse a directory a descriptor or a working
directory refers to, after the check for emptiness.
tmpfs removes such a directory, as Linux does, and leaves its
descriptors and working directories on an empty directory that takes no
new entry; POSIX permits either answer.
.Pp
Relative paths are resolved from the current working directory.
Both calls require search permission on ancestor directories and
write permission on the parent.
The owner, group, and other permission bits are checked using the
caller's effective IDs; effective user ID 0 overrides these checks.
The KRFFS root directory is treated as mode 0755 and non-sticky.
.Pp
In a sticky directory,
.Fn rmdir
also requires ownership of the entry, ownership of the parent, or
effective user ID 0.
Write permission on the entry alone does not permit removal.
The parent write check precedes the sticky check.
.Pp
Neither call changes the synthetic namespaces under
.Pa /dev
and
.Pa /proc .
Changes on a read-only mount return
.Er EROFS .
A name that exists, in those namespaces or on a read-only mount, returns
.Er EEXIST
first, as on Linux.
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 39 for
.Fn mkdir ,
296 for
.Fn mkdirat
and 40 for
.Fn rmdir .
.Fn mkdir
and
.Fn rmdir
take the path in EBX, and
.Fn mkdir
the mode in ECX;
.Fn mkdirat
takes the directory descriptor, the path and the mode in EBX, ECX and
EDX.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions return 0 on success, or -1 on failure and set
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/dirprobe.c
creates
.Pa /d
with mode 0755, checks that another creation fails, and creates a file
inside it.
It checks that removal of the nonempty directory fails, then removes
the file and the empty directory.
On a KRFFS root it creates
.Pa /ids/bC
and checks that
.Pa /ids/cb
is refused with
.Er EINVAL .
.Pa tests/fdstale.c
holds a directory by a descriptor opened for reading, then by one opened
with
.Dv O_PATH ,
then as a child's working directory, then as its own, checks that
.Fn rmdir
returns
.Er EBUSY
each time, and removes the directory once it has gone back to the root
and nothing refers to the directory.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ENAMETOOLONG]"
.It Bq Er EACCES
Search permission on an ancestor or write permission on the parent
was denied.
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fn mkdirat
was given a relative path and a
.Fa dirfd
that is not open.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
Path resolution entered a mount being mounted or unmounted, or
.Fn rmdir
selected the root, a mount point, a process's root directory, or a
directory in use: one a descriptor refers to, or on the file systems
that count them, a process's working directory.
.It Bq Er EEXIST
The name passed to
.Fn mkdir
already exists, including the root and mount points, or its last
component is
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&.. .
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The path is null or cannot be read from user memory, or there is no
current process.
.It Bq Er EFBIG
An ext2 directory cannot grow further or a required block exceeds its
addressing limit.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fn rmdir
was given a path whose last component is
.Pa \&. ,
the file system rejects a new name, KRFFS among them for a directory whose
identifier another directory has, or required attributes cannot be
obtained.
.It Bq Er EIO
The backing device was removed, or file system metadata could not be
read or written.
.It Bq Er ELOOP
Path resolution would follow more than 40 symbolic links.
.It Bq Er EMLINK
The ext2 parent already has 65000 links.
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
A path, component, reconstructed parent path, or symbolic-link
expansion is too long.
The copied path and its working-directory prefix must fit 4096 bytes,
including the terminating null byte.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
The path is empty, an ancestor is missing, no root is mounted, or
the entry to remove is absent on a file system that reports this error.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The file system could not allocate a node or other required memory.
.It Bq Er ENOSPC
There is no space for the new directory or its parent entry.
.It Bq Er ENOTDIR
An ancestor is not a directory,
.Fn mkdirat
was given a relative path and a
.Fa dirfd
that is not open on a directory, or
.Fn rmdir
selected a non-directory on a file system that reports this error.
.It Bq Er ENOTEMPTY
The directory is not empty, or the last component of the path given to
.Fn rmdir
is
.Pa \&.. .
A mounted KRFFS volume also uses this error for a missing final entry
or a final entry that is not a directory.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The sticky-directory rule forbids removal.
.It Bq Er EROFS
The operation requires a change to a read-only mount or synthetic
namespace, or the file system does not implement the operation.
exFAT also refuses removal of a directory it has marked unwritable.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chmod 2 ,
.Xr mount 2 ,
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr umask 2 ,
.Xr unlink 2
